<section><title>Video 1 review questions</title>
<!--  <p>Coming soon!</p> -->
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Consider a population of bacteria that is modeled by the state variable <m>P</m>. In the equations below, <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> are functions, and <m>c</m> is a constant. Which equation expresses the idea that the change in <m>P</m> over a single time step is a linear function of the population?
  <answer>
    <choice><m>P_{t+1} = g(P_t)</m></choice>
    <choice><m>P_{t+1} - P_t = h(P_t)</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"><m>P_{t+1} - P_t = c P_t</m></choice>
  </answer>
</p>

  <feedback condition="$_choice1"><p>This is the general formula for a dynamical system written in <term><ref to="doenet:82YaRL8CieueYqLgxMpxye">function iteration form</ref></term>. If we knew the function <m>g</m> we could transform the equation into difference form and evaluate whether we arrive at a linear function for the change. But we can't perform this check without knowing the function <m>g(P_t)</m>.</p> 
<p>You can find a hint to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 2</ref> on the video.</p></feedback>

  <feedback condition="$_choice2"><p>This is the general formula for a dynamical system written in <term><ref to="doenet:832kteFVLDpZJ9tARy27gK">difference form</ref></term>. This <em>could</em> be the correct response, if we were sure that the unknown function, <m>h(P_t)</m>, just happens to be a constant number multiplied by <m>P_t</m>. But we can't tell this for sure without knowing the function <m>h(P_t)</m>.</p>
<p> You can find a hint to this question at <ref to="$vid_section" name="buttonLink2">Key Moment 2</ref> on the video.</p></feedback>

<feedback condition="$_choice3">
<p> Exactly! Since the coefficent <m>c</m> of <m>P_t</m> on the right-hand side of this equation does <em>not</em> depend on <m>t</m> or <m>P_t</m>, we can tell that the change in <m>P</m> from time <m>t</m> to time <m>(t + 1)</m> is a linear function.</p>
    <p>Additionally, since there is no constant term on the right-hand side (compare the equation to <m>y = mx + b</m>, where "<m>y</m>"<m> = (P_{t+1} - P_t)</m>, "<m>c</m>"<m> = m</m> and the <m>b</m> is zero...), we can tell that this linear function passes through the origin, as it should.</p>
    <p>Why should it pass through the origin? Well, if the bacteria population at time <m>t</m> is zero (<m>P_t</m> on the horizontal axis), then the change in bacteria population over one time step <m>(P_{t+1} - P{t})</m> is also 0; in other words, we cannot grow bacteria from nothing!</p>
</feedback>

<solution>
  <me>P_{t+1} - P_t = c P_t</me>
<p>Since the coefficent <m>c</m> of <m>P_t</m> on the right-hand side of this equation does <em>not</em> depend on <m>t</m> or <m>P_t</m>, we can tell that the change in <m>P</m> from time <m>t</m> to time <m>(t + 1)</m> is a linear function.</p></solution>
</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>In the model developed in the video, the change in bacteria population (at least for the first four time steps) was found to be well-described by the equation
  <me>B_{t+1} - B_t = \frac{2}{3}B_t.</me>
</p>
<p>Which of the choices below is the best way to explain the meaning of the number <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> in this equation?</p>
<p>
  <answer>
    <choice credit="1"><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> represents the fraction of the bacteria population that divides over one time step</choice>
    <choice><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> represents how long it takes all of the bacteria to divide one time.</choice>
    <choice><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> represents the fraction of bacteria initially present at time <m>t = 0</m>.</choice>
  </answer>
</p>

<feedback condition="$_choice4">Nice work! Yes, we can see this fact even clearer if we convert the equation to <term><ref to="doenet:82YaRL8CieueYqLgxMpxye">function iteration form</ref></term> as follows: Add <m>B_t</m> to both sides, and we get
  <me>B_{t+1} = (1 + \frac{2}{3})B_t</me>
In other words, the amount of bacteria in time step <m>(t+1)</m> is just equal to the same amount as in the previous time step, <m>1 \cdot B_t</m>, with an additional <m>\frac{2}{3} \cdot B_t</m> (and this fraction <m>\frac{2}{3} \cdot B_t</m> corresponds to all of the bacteria that decided it was time for them to assexually reproduce themselves during that time step...)</feedback>

<feedback condition="$_choice5">
<p>Remember, if we were to graph the equation <m>B_{t+1} - B_t = \frac{2}{3}B_t</m>, we would have the quantity <m>(B_{t+1} - B_t)</m> or the <m>change</m> in bacteria population during one time step on the vertical or "<m>y</m>" axis, and the population density at time <m>t</m>, or <m>B_t</m> on the horizontal or "<m>x</m>" axis.</p>
  <image source="http://mathinsight.org/media/image/image/bacteria_density_change_versus_density.png"><shortDescription>Changes in experimental measurements of bacteria population density are plotted versus the population density at the beginning of the interval.</shortDescription></image>
<p>You can find a hint to this question at <ref to="$vid_section">Key Moment 1</ref> on the video.</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<feedback condition="$_choice6" extend="$_feedback5" />
<solution><m>\frac{2}{3}</m> represents the fraction of the bacteria population that divides over one time step</solution>
</section>





